Z||000008||Ken Wittlief||08-03-2001||05:16 PM||||sure there are exceptions, but everytime you start and drive a car the bearings and the valve guides, and the pistons and the rings, and the moving parts in the tranny wear<P> you simply cannot drive a car 260,000 miles without a lot of wear happening, no matter how often you change the oil.<P>  if you find one that was babied, ie, the owner almost never tramped down on the gas pedal, never reved it over 2,500 RPM, then yes it will last a long time, but the rings will still have worn the cyliner walls in a pattern, and the first time you rev it up to 5,000 RPM you start eating the rings.<P>  so you cant have it both ways, a sporty car like the fiero that has been driven like a sports car needs major drive train work done long before you get to 260,000 miles.<P>  and if its been babied its whole life then it wont last very long if you buy it and start driving it like it was meant to be driven.<P>   law of physics are still in effect as of this morning - moving metal parts wear out - there is no free lunch.<P>  and one of the saddest things to see is a car that looks really great, has been pampered, but has high miles - you just know it needs serious mechanical work - but it looks soooooo nice - very easy to get suckered into.||||reg||10
